Below is a quick‑start guide on **how to pull out every `<Element>` child** from a `Node` that represents a SAML assertion (`SAMLAssertionNode`).  
I’ll cover two ways:

1. **Using the built‑in DOM APIs directly** – handy when you’re writing your own utility method.  
2. **Calling the supplied helper (`SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren`)** – useful if you already have that wrapper available in your test harness.

---

## 1️⃣ Plain‑Java DOM approach  

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import org.w3c.dom.*;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Utility that returns *all* direct child Elements of a given Node.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
public static List<Element> getDirectChildElements(Node parent) {

    List<Element> elementChildren = new ArrayList<>();

    // Grab the raw child node list first
    NodeList childNodes = parent.getChildNodes();

    // Iterate over each child node
    for (int i = 0; i < childNodes.getLength(); i++) {
        Node current = childNodes.item(i);

        // Only keep those that are actual Element instances
        if (current instanceof Element) {
            elementChildren.add((Element) current);
        }
    }

    return elementChildren;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example usage inside some larger routine that has parsed a SAML doc:
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
public void demo() throws Exception {
    DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
    dbf.setNamespaceAware(true);          // Important for SAML!
    Document doc = dbf.newDocumentBuilder()
                       .parse(new File("saml_assertion.xml"));

    // Assuming the root is our SAMLAssertionNode:
    Node samlRoot = doc.getDocumentElement();   // e.g., <samlp:Response ...>

    List<Element> childEls = getDirectChildElements(samlRoot);

    System.out.println("Found " + childEls.size() + " Element children:");
    for (Element el : childEls) {
        System.out.printf("- <%s>%n", el.getTagName());
    }
}
```

### Why this works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| `parent.getChildNodes()` | Returns a live `NodeList` containing **every** child node (elements, text, comments, etc.). |
| `instanceof Element` | Filters only those nodes whose runtime class implements `org.w3c.dom.Element`. |
| Cast & add | Safely casts back to `Element` so callers can work with tag names, attributes, namespaces, etc. |

This pattern is idiomatic across almost all Java XML libraries (DOM, JAXP, Xerces, etc.).

---

## 2️⃣ Using the helper function `SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren`

Your environment exposes a convenience wrapper named `SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren`, presumably part of an automated testing framework. It abstracts away the boilerplate above into one line.

